    {
      "_id": "d9H1XYaZN2RC3VqL",
      "_key": "!journal.pages!gRfqeOIXEHOcLemr.d9H1XYaZN2RC3VqL",
      "name": "Mortal Days",
      "type": "text",
      "title": {
        "show": true,
        "level": 1
      },
      "text": {
        "format": 1,
        "content": "<p>Ruth performed autopsies for twelve years. She was meticulous, dry, and had no patience for the living. In 2004 she signed the report on a body with no blood and no visible wounds. The next day the body had vanished from the morgue cooler, and her boss asked her to “revise” the report.</p>\n"
      },
      "sort": 200000,
      "ownership": {
        "default": 2
      },
      "flags": {
        "seattle-by-night": {
          "origen": "pagina"
        }
      },
      "_stats": {
        "coreVersion": "14.365",
        "systemId": "wod5e",
        "systemVersion": "5.3.28"
      }
    },
    {
      "_id": "pAxQHkWxPX6KBSyP",
      "_key": "!journal.pages!gRfqeOIXEHOcLemr.pAxQHkWxPX6KBSyP",
      "name": "Kindred Nights",
      "type": "text",
      "title": {
        "show": true,
        "level": 1
      },
      "text": {
        "format": 1,
        "content": "<p>Dr. Anneliese Voight (@UUID[JournalEntry.yaAsxCkCLf8CANL5]{Dr. Anneliese Voight}) Embraced her in 2005: a medical examiner who asks questions is a danger, but a medical examiner who answers to the chantry is a weapon. Since then, Ruth is the reason Kindred deaths in Seattle end up as “overdose,” “accidental fire,” or “animal attack.” This week she&#39;s on leave: a substitute is signing the night-shift reports. She feeds in hospitals, on sedated patients.</p>\n"
      },
      "sort": 300000,
      "ownership": {
        "default": 2
      },
      "flags": {
        "seattle-by-night": {
          "origen": "pagina"
        }
      },
